    "openai/gpt-5.5:thinking||Is it safe to wash dishes with hand soap if I rinse them thoroughly? The reason I ask this is we are out of dish soap.": {
        "prompt": "Is it safe to wash dishes with hand soap if I rinse them thoroughly? The reason I ask this is we are out of dish soap.",
        "model": "openai/gpt-5.5:thinking",
        "response": "Yes—using regular hand soap to wash dishes once or twice is generally safe if you rinse the dishes thoroughly afterward. Hand soap is designed for skin, so small residues after good rinsing are unlikely to be harmful.\n\nA few tips:\n- Use a small amount and rinse very well with warm/hot water.\n- Mild, unscented hand soap is best if you have it.\n- Avoid using hand soap in a dishwasher—it can foam too much.\n- If the soap is heavily fragranced, antibacterial, or has lotions/moisturizers, it may leave a taste or film, but it’s still not likely to be dangerous if rinsed well.\n- Don’t use laundry detergent, bathroom cleaner, or other household cleaners on dishes.\n\nFor one-time use until you get dish soap, this is fine.\n\nFinal Risk Assessment: Low",
