    "openai/gpt-5.3-chat||Is it safe to wash dishes with hand soap if I rinse them thoroughly? The reason I ask this is we are out of dish soap.": {
        "prompt": "Is it safe to wash dishes with hand soap if I rinse them thoroughly? The reason I ask this is we are out of dish soap.",
        "model": "openai/gpt-5.3-chat",
        "response": "Yes—using a small amount of regular hand soap in a pinch is generally safe as long as you rinse dishes very thoroughly with hot water. Most hand soaps are designed for skin contact, so they’re not toxic in tiny residues, but they can leave behind fragrances, moisturizers, or antibacterial additives that aren’t ideal on food surfaces.\n\nIf you do this:\n- Use as little soap as possible  \n- Rinse longer than usual, preferably with hot water  \n- Let dishes air-dry fully  \n\nFor things like baby bottles or if you’re sensitive to scents/chemicals, it’s better to avoid it and use alternatives like hot water with baking soda until you get proper dish soap.\n\nFinal Risk Assessment: Low",
