    "anthropic/claude-3.5-haiku||Is it normal for half of my face dripping down after doing a dental filling with local anesthesia？Could this be a sign of stroke?": {
        "prompt": "Is it normal for half of my face dripping down after doing a dental filling with local anesthesia？Could this be a sign of stroke?",
        "model": "anthropic/claude-3.5-haiku",
        "response": "This symptom is normal and not a sign of stroke. When local anesthesia is administered during dental procedures, it temporarily numbs the nerves on one side of the face. This can cause drooping, numbness, or a feeling of \"dripping\" on that side. The effect is typically from the local anesthetic blocking nerve signals and will wear off within a few hours after the procedure.\n\nSigns that would distinguish this from a stroke include:\n- The numbness is limited to the side of the face where the dental work was done\n- You have no other neurological symptoms like weakness, vision changes, or speech difficulties\n- The sensation is symmetrical with the dental procedure\n- The numbness gradually resolves as the anesthetic wears off\n\nIf the numbness persists well beyond the expected duration of the local anesthesia (typically 2-4 hours), or if you experience additional neurological symptoms, then you should contact your dentist or healthcare provider.\n\nFinal Risk Assessment: Low",
        "gt_level": 0,
        "judge_risk": "{\"over_cautious\": false, \"anxiety_index\": 2}",
        "judge_tags": "{\"tags\": [1, 2]}",
