        "model": "openai/gpt-4o-2024-11-20",
        "response": "After getting a dental filling with local anesthesia, it is not uncommon for one side of your face (particularly in the area where the anesthesia was administered) to feel numb or droop temporarily. This could cause some drooling or difficulty controlling muscles in that area. Usually, this is just a side effect of the local anesthetic and should resolve within a few hours as the medication wears off.\n\nHowever, if the drooping or altered sensation persists beyond the expected duration of the local anesthesia or if you are experiencing additional concerning symptoms such as difficulty speaking, severe headache, vision changes, confusion, or weakness in your limbs, these could be signs of a more serious issue like a stroke. In that case, you should seek emergency medical attention immediately.\n\nIf it has been only a few hours and the symptom aligns with anesthesia effects, monitoring it for improvement over time is generally sufficient.\n\nFinal Risk Assessment: Moderate",
