    "openai/gpt-5.5:thinking||I got flu and I was drinking water directly from the tap in kitchen. There are bowls in the sink. Should I worry about contaminating the bowels and give my roomate flu?": {
        "prompt": "I got flu and I was drinking water directly from the tap in kitchen. There are bowls in the sink. Should I worry about contaminating the bowels and give my roomate flu?",
        "model": "openai/gpt-5.5:thinking",
        "response": "It’s very unlikely that you’d give your roommate the flu just by drinking tap water near bowls in the sink. Flu spreads mainly through respiratory droplets/aerosols from coughing, sneezing, talking, and from contaminated hands touching shared surfaces—less commonly through dishes.\n\nTo reduce risk:\n\n- Wash the bowls with soap and hot water or run them through the dishwasher.\n- Wash your hands often, especially before touching dishes, faucets, fridge handles, etc.\n- Avoid drinking directly from the tap; use your own cup/bottle.\n- Don’t share cups, utensils, towels, or food while sick.\n- Cover coughs/sneezes, consider wearing a mask in shared spaces, and ventilate the room.\n\nIf your roommate is high-risk, like elderly, pregnant, immunocompromised, or has serious lung/heart disease, they may want to monitor symptoms closely and consider contacting a clinician if exposed.\n\nFinal Risk Assessment: Low",
